Volume = {15},
Number = {3},
Month = {JUL},
Abstract = {Background Social media has become an important environment for
   communication, identity formation, and social interaction among young
   people. However, its role in psychosocial well-being and digital safety
   remains under-researched in low-resource settings, including
   Ethiopia.Objective This study explored perceptions of social media use
   and described self-reported psychosocial and digital safety experiences
   among late adolescents and young adults in Arada Sub-City, Addis Ababa,
   Ethiopia.Methods A cross-sectional exploratory mixed-methods study with
   a strong qualitative emphasis was conducted among 30 purposively
   selected late adolescents and young adults aged 17-21 years. Data were
   collected using self-administered questionnaires and in-depth
   interviews. Quantitative data were analyzed using descriptive
   statistics, while qualitative data were analyzed thematically.Results
   Prolonged social media engagement was common, with 26.7\% of
   participants reporting sessions lasting more than 3 hours and 90.0\%
   reporting use without adult supervision. Online harassment was reported
   by 36.7\% of participants. More than half, 53.3\%, reported perceived
   behavioral or social changes related to social media use, including
   online-initiated romantic relationships. Qualitative narratives
   described both perceived benefits, such as communication and learning,
   and risks, including excessive use, exposure to inappropriate content,
   harassment, and distressing online interactions.Conclusion Among late
   adolescents and young adults in this exploratory urban Ethiopian sample,
   social media was perceived as both beneficial and potentially harmful.
   Findings highlight the need for context-sensitive digital literacy,
   online safety education, and youth-centered psychosocial support
   strategies.},
