Title = {Bamboo-based traditional bamboo market systems enhance rural livelihoods
   and ecosystem resilience in Karnataka},
Journal = {FRONTIERS IN ECOLOGY AND EVOLUTION},
Year = {2026},
Volume = {14},
Month = {JUL 31},
Abstract = {Bamboo, referred to as ``green gold,{''} is a significant long-term
   resource for generating a green economy through its socio-ecological,
   cultural, and livelihood contributions, including carbon sequestration,
   landscape restoration, and biodiversity support. This research examines
   the dynamics of Traditional Bamboo Products (TBP) within Traditional
   Bamboo Markets (TBM) and their vital role in sustaining rural
   livelihoods, local economies, and ecosystem resilience across Karnataka.
   A mixed-methods research approach was employed from 2017 to 2026 across
   eight districts of Karnataka (Mysuru, Chamarajanagar, Uttara Kannada,
   Shivamogga, Kodagu, Belagavi, Ballari, and Bidar). A stratified sampling
   approach was used with 16 respondents from each district, representing
   Traditional Bamboo Artisans (TBA), Adivasi and tribal communities, and
   small-scale entrepreneurs. Primary data collection involved
   semi-structured questionnaires capturing socio-economic and demographic
   characteristics, bamboo procurement sources, harvesting practices, and
   marketing strategies. Secondary data included population, literacy
   trends, and ecological stress indicators. Analysis combined comparative
   and descriptive methods with multivariable linear regression examining
   forest cover, annual rainfall, and forest area as predictors. Key
   findings revealed that bamboo dependence increases with proximity to
   forests, and better market access enables value addition, enterprise
   diversification, and strengthens indigenous knowledge systems. Bamboo
   availability varies across three forest types-moist deciduous, dry
   deciduous, and thorn/scrub forests-with different land-use patterns. A
   four-zone Bamboo Value Chain Model (BVCM) was developed based on field
   observations, covering forest, rural, semi-urban, and craft-based
   systems, identifying market integration patterns at local scales through
   spatial zonation. The BVCM provides a quantitative and spatial framework
   for regional planning, market development, and sustainable management.
   The model highlights zone-specific strengths and gaps, enabling targeted
   interventions. Stronger policy support and institutional frameworks are
   essential for long-term sustainability across Karnataka.},
